#X text 79 488 Incoming value to tally in the histogram, f 15;
#X text 239 518 The histogram object below stores all the histogram data in a [text] object. In order to successfully add new entries to the [text] storage \, we need to write to an index beyond which has already been written \, so we use a very large index number. This inlet sets this large index number \, and so we take the size of the histogram/array (i.e. the largest number to tally) and add 1e+06 to it \, so that we are always correctly appending values., f 22;
#X text 439 648 Symbol filepath sets a file to be loaded \, bang opens a "load" dialog, f 15;
#X text 569 498 bang to save teh contents of the histogram to a file, f 15;
#X text 899 498 if we resize the array \, its a good idea to reload the contents of the histogram, f 15;
#X text 1229 498 clear histogram and array, f 15;
#X text 1099 1018 when a file is loaded or saved \, this outlet reports the filepath. When the filepath is entered via the GUI \, then the filepath is displayed already \, but when its loaded via external msg or open/save dialog \, the path needs to get reported to the GUI for display. We use "set" here so that the GUI gets updated \, but it doesn't also output the path \, because that output would then cause the path to be loaded again \, then reported again \, then loaded again \, creating an infinite loop and crash.;
#X text 1119 1178 because we don't output the path from the GUI in some scenarios \, we grab the path here to send to the [savestate] mechanism so that it gets saved and loaded if "Embed" is enabled., f 43;
#X text 19 938 each incoming value causes the histogram object to update the value's instances tally \, and report that value and tally. This outlet formats it so that it can be displayed in an array., f 38;
#X text 249 958 --->;
#X text 539 948 <---;
#X text 579 938 when histogram gets dumped all at once \, it comes out of this outlet. [\$2 \$1] rearranges the order so that it will properly be loaded into an array., f 35;
#X text 219 1318 treats the histogram array like a probability table. A random histogram value will get output \, whose probability of output is based on the number of instances the value has had.;
#X text 229 1398 Get the number of instances for a specific value in the histogram, f 33;
#X text 2279 638 This sets the displayed label for the [cnv] object used to display the x axis value below the array. For some reason just supplying a float to replace "\$1" in [label \$1( produces an error \, so we use [makefilename] to format the incoming float and then it doesn't complain.;
